title: Gate report
tags: TW5Syntax GrammarData
caption: Gate report

Seventeen instruments stand in `tools/`, and each prints a line nobody keeps. A reader who wants
the grammar's state runs them all and reads scrollback; a reader who opens this wiki sees the
rulings that govern them and nothing about whether they hold.

<<lar-link "$:/tw5-syntax/GateReport">> carries what each gate said when it last ran.

! A harvest, and what that costs

No hand edits it, and it goes stale the moment the tree moves. That reads as a feature: a report
agreeing with a tree it no longer describes would offer reassurance rather than a reading.

`npm run gates -- --check` runs them again and compares. It reports two different things, and the
difference matters:

|!Reading |!Meaning |
|a gate ''does not hold'' |the tree has a finding the gate names |
|the report ''DRIFTED'' |the tree moved and this page describes the tree before it |

A gate can hold while the report drifts — somebody fixed something and never re-harvested — and a
gate can fail while the report stands current, which names a real finding.

! Where the list comes from

The manifest, never this page. A gate somebody adds joins the report without anybody remembering,
and a gate that leaves drops out of the report rather than reporting a stale pass. What stands aside:
a builder, a server, a reporting tool that answers a question rather than judging one, and the
per-scope snapshot runs that `snap` already carries whole.

! What a red gate means here

Not every red names a defect, and the report shows each rather than hiding it.

|!Gate |!Why it reads red |
|`overreach` |it measures spans where the grammar and the parser diverge, and reports rather than judges |
|`canary-wikitext` `compose-wikitext` |a specimen carries an unterminated styleblock, and TiddlyWiki swallows one to the end of the source — `void [styleblock] @0-28` — so the grammar agrees with the parser and the canary reads the agreement as a bleed |
|`nesting-coverage` `upstream-coverage` |the manifest runs them with no argument, and each wants a path to a TiddlyWiki |

A gate that answers a question rather than judging one wants moving out of this report or giving
its argument, and a canary that cannot tell a legitimate swallow from a leak wants the parser's
answer beside its own. Both stand recorded rather than silenced.

! One thing this report caught on its first run

`corpus` read red at 13 unreached scopes against a ceiling of 3, and the sweep that had reported
it green each turn had read the FLOOR line and never the ceiling. The unreached scopes came from
this session — a system title, a regexp operand and its flags, a built-in variable — and the corpus
exercised none of them. Two specimens close them, and the floor stands at 466.
